# Explicit dependencies; avoid mixins when practical

## Prefer direct calls over `include` / `extend`

When you only need a few helpers from another module, **expose them as module methods or class methods** (e.g. `SomeModule.do_thing(...)`) and **call them from the caller** instead of mixing the module into your class with `include`.

- Makes dependencies obvious at the call site (better for humans and AI).
- Reserve `include` for APIs that truly need instance-level integration (e.g. framework hooks).

```ruby
# ❌ Avoid when a single function-style helper is enough
class MyClient
  include HttpHelpers
  def fetch
    send_request(...)
  end
end

# ✅ Prefer explicit module API
class MyClient
  def fetch
    HttpHelpers.send_request(...)
  end
end
```

Use normal **subclassing** only when an “is-a” relationship and shared instance behavior justify it; do not add abstract base classes just to share a method.

## Comment dependencies on other classes/modules

When calling **another class or module’s method** (especially from a different namespace), add a **short comment** naming **which type** you depend on and **which method** (and briefly why if non-obvious).

```ruby
# Delegates HTTP and request logging to Llm::RequestSender.send_request.
response = Llm::RequestSender.send_request(openrouter_uri: uri, ...)
```

Keep comments one line when possible; skip only when the call is already fully self-describing *and* in the same small file with no indirection.
